09:49:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your teacher is right that they are **different notes in musical meaning**, even though on a normally tuned modern piano they are played using the **same key**. “Same key” does not necessarily mean “same note” in the theoretical or spelling sense. - **C♯** is a kind of C: it is “C raised.” - **D♭** is a kind of D: it is “D lowered.” That distinction affects intervals, scales, chord spelling, harmonic analysis, and how a musician reads and performs a line. For example: - C to C♯ is an **augmented unison**: some form of C moving upward. - C to D♭ is a **minor second**: C moving to some form of D. On a 12-tone-equal-tempered piano, both are played by the same black key, so their physical sounding frequency is the same. But musically, they are not interchangeable in notation. For instance, in D major, the leading tone is **C♯**, because it is the seventh scale degree rising to D: \[ D\quad E\quad F♯\quad G\quad A\quad B\quad C♯\quad D \] Calling that C♯ a D♭ would obscure its role—it is functioning as an altered C that wants to resolve upward to D, not as a lowered D. Likewise: - A **C♯ major** triad is C♯–E♯–G♯. - A **D♭ major** triad is D♭–F–A♭. Those are distinct spellings and harmonic ideas, despite being the same three piano keys in equal temperament. And in tuning systems other than modern equal temperament, your teacher’s statement can be literally acoustically true too: C♯ and D♭ may be tuned to different frequencies. A standard fixed-pitch piano generally cannot make that distinction, because it has one key/string set for both names. So the concise version is: - **On a standard modern piano:** C♯ and D♭ normally have the same frequency and key. - **In musical theory and notation:** they are different notes, with different functions. - **In some non-equal tuning systems:** they can also be physically different pitches.
