09:54:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. In equal temperament, it is not a matter of rounding two “true” hidden pitches to the same measured value. It is a tuning rule that divides the octave into 12 equal frequency ratios: \[ f_{n+1}=f_n \times 2^{1/12} \] Under that rule, there is one pitch position between C and D. Both the spellings **C♯** and **D♭** are assigned to that same position. Therefore, in ideal 12-tone equal temperament, they are **exactly the same frequency by definition**, not merely approximately equal after rounding. For example, if A4 is defined as 440 Hz, then the shared C♯4/D♭4 pitch is: \[ 440 \times 2^{-8/12} \approx 277.18263098\text{ Hz} \] There are not two separate ET values lurking behind that number. What *is* true is: - C♯ and D♭ retain different **musical identities**: raised C versus lowered D. - In other temperaments, they can be assigned distinct frequency positions. - In live performance, violinists and singers may intentionally realize them differently. - A real piano string will not be mathematically perfect anyway—it may be a few cents off due to tuning and physical effects—but that is ordinary tuning variation, not two underlying pitches being rounded together. So: equal temperament merges the acoustic pitch positions deliberately; it does not conceal a pre-existing C♯/D♭ frequency difference through measurement rounding.
