09:55:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You generally should not single out C♯/D♭. The same distinction applies to every enharmonic pair: - C♯ / D♭ - D♯ / E♭ - F♯ / G♭ - G♯ / A♭ - A♯ / B♭ In key signatures and theory, **G♯ major and A♭ major are differently spelled keys**, with different written scales, chord spellings, and conventional usage—even though 12-TET makes their tonic pitches coincide. For instance: - G♯ major: G♯ A♯ B♯ C♯ D♯ E♯ F𝄪 - A♭ major: A♭ B♭ C D♭ E♭ F G In practice, A♭ major is far more readable, so it is normally used instead of G♯ major. The frequency caveat belongs only when someone asks an acoustic question such as “How many Hz apart are they?” In a discussion of key signatures, the useful answer is simply that the spellings—and therefore the keys’ theoretical identities—are different.
