09:56:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes: within notation, **C♯ and D♭ are distinct named notes**. They belong to different letter families: - C♯ is a modified C. - D♭ is a modified D. That distinction is essential throughout the notation system: it preserves correct scale spelling, interval names, chord construction, voice-leading, and key relationships. The only qualification is that notation can map distinct names to the same pitch in a particular tuning convention. In 12-tone equal temperament, C♯ and D♭ are enharmonic: distinct names, one pitch location. That does not make the notation distinction disappear.
